    "data": {
      "A": "Research an exact, work-efficient sequential algorithm. Build a sparse ID-to-row index, resolve each row's chosen successor once, and memoize downstream results across queries. Preserve first-match lookup behavior without assuming dense, sorted or positive IDs. Use iterative traversal and reverse unwinding to avoid recursion-depth limits while reproducing each original float addition; terminal nodes must return their value unchanged. Use separate cache-state flags, not numeric result sentinels. Target expected O(n + m + q) work with hashing, where m counts neighbor references and q counts queries. Measure against the unchanged baseline through the existing harness.",
      "B": "Research low-overhead input, representation and sparse-query processing. Compare buffered, line-aware parsing and compact arrays against fscanf, copied neighbor strings and repeated strtok_r/atoi calls. Preserve float conversion accuracy, query token spelling, line boundaries and output formatting. Compare compact sorted ID lookup with hashing without assuming contiguous IDs. Explore lazy successor resolution and memoization restricted to query-reachable rows, avoiding global edge-resolution work when little of the graph is queried. Separate parser/layout improvements from lazy-processing improvements where the existing measurement budget permits.",
      "C": "Determine whether CPU parallelism actually pays at this short runtime. Using only facilities supported by the unchanged build, evaluate coarse-grained successor construction across at most four workers, followed by exact dependency-respecting evaluation. Alternatives include independent-query workers with private traversal/cache state; compare their duplicated work against serial shared memoization. Keep lookup structures immutable during parallel reads, avoid partially published cache entries, and preserve query output order. Do not parallel-reduce or reassociate path sums. Retain a sequential path for small workloads or narrow dependency chains, and prefer it if synchronization and worker startup erase gains.",
      "G": "Integrate only validated programs produced by A/B/C. Establish each candidate's standalone correctness and timing, then measure selected combinations rather than assuming gains add. Reconcile parser ownership, ID indexing, successor representation and cache lifetime so integration does not introduce duplicate preprocessing or races. Audit immediate-value selection, strict ties, absent IDs, the -1 stop sentinel, per-node float rounding and literal query labels. Use existing correctness checks and repeated timing measurements within the fixed budget; a few correctness cases are not a general proof. Keep the fastest supported valid standalone candidate if integration regresses. Do not change tests, oracle, compiler, target or budgets.",
      "analysis": "The workload is Problem F in src/graph.c. The only measured program is the correct baseline: mean 14.900 ms over three samples, ranging from 14.666 to 15.317 ms, with four correctness cases. No optimization gain or runtime breakdown has been established. Static inspection identifies repeated linear ID searches, repeated neighbor tokenization and recomputation of shared suffixes as opportunities. This is not a maximum-total-weight DAG path: each node chooses the first listed, existing neighbor whose immediate float value strictly exceeds the running maximum, initially zero, then follows only that neighbor. Missing queried IDs return zero; the original -1 stopping check must remain. Preserve the recursive float addition order and unchanged terminal values, six-decimal formatting, query order, duplicates and original query token spelling. ARM64 has four CPU cores and no GPU; approximately 15 ms total runtime makes overhead and measurement variability important."
    },
